     1	# Statuses
     2	
     3	9 statuses organized in a workflow:
     4	
     5	| Status | Meaning | Who sets |
     6	|--------|---------|----------|
     7	| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |
     8	| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |
     9	| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |
    10	| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |
    11	| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |
    12	| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |
    13	| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | reviewer / orchestrator after reviewer verdict; parent aggregation |
    14	| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |
    15	| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |
    16	
    17	## Status Flow
    18	
    19	**Happy path:**
    20	```
    21	backlog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → done
    22	```
    23	
    24	**Returns from reviewing:**
    25	- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research
    26	- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework
    27	
    28	**Terminal from any status:**
    29	- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled
    30	- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision
    31	
    32	**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved, and the item can be reopened if needed.
    33	
    34	For a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`
    35	means it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be
    36	closed from any lifecycle state.
    37	
    38	For a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is
    39	`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the
    40	container itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and
    41	transitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also
    42	satisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close
    43	containers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports
    44	`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active
    45	descendants.
    46	
    47	**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.
    48	
    49	**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:
    50	1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.
    51	2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.
    52	3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.
    53	4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.
    54	5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.
    55	6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.
    56	7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.
    57	
    58	`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.
    59	
    60	`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.
    61	
    62	Human-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means "ready for review" or "handed off to review"; it is not "done", "complete", "finished", "final", or "готово". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.
    63	
    64	## Per-Element Review Policy
    65	
    66	Every board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with
    67	`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with
    68	`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query
    69	projection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to
    70	`required`.
    71	
    72	- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an
    73	  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.
    74	- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.
    75	- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`
    76	  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,
    77	  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the
    78	  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.
    79	
    80	This policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and
    81	never introduces routine human review.
    82	
    83	## blocked vs is_blocked
    84	
    85	Two different concepts:
    86	
    87	- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.
    88	- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked
    89	
    90	CLI display:
    91	```
    92	TASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency
    93	TASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status
    94	```
    95	
    96	## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation
    97	
    98	After a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct
    99	children. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an
   100	assigned child without implying ownership of the container.
   101	
   102	- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.
   103	  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do
   104	  not silently normalize it to `done`.
   105	- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:
   106	  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.
   107	  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked
   108	  children keeps its current manual status.
   109	- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then
   110	  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not
   111	  recomputed.
   112	
   113	```bash
   114	task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'
   115	# TASK-260205-abc123 → development
   116	# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)
   117	# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)
   118	```
   119	
   120	For Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into
   121	`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with
   122	`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal
   123	is a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start
   124	refusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are
   125	grandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for
   126	an element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.
   127	
   128	### Commit confirmation for accepted work
   129	
   130	Top-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to
   131	`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation
   132	that would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local
   133	acknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story
   134	transition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its
   135	parent Story.
   136	
   137	```json
   138	{
   139	  "version_control": {
   140	    "confirm": true,
   141	    "desired_commit_time": "evening MSK"
   142	  }
   143	}
   144	```
   145	
   146	```bash
   147	task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'
   148	```
   149	
   150	The error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story
   151	transition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The
   152	acknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is
   153	allowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Local evaluation and
   154	status writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first
   155	requires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then
   156	the server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses
   157	under one serialized mutation.
   158	
   159	A reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`. It records
   160	acceptance evidence and hands it to the mover that owns the scoped commit; that
   161	mover commits first, then makes the final `done` transition with
   162	`commit_ack=scope_committed`.
   163	
   164	Reactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in
   165	`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status
   166	runs ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. Any `done` or `closed`
   167	container on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;
   168	explicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target
   169	is the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:
   170	
   171	```bash
   172	task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'
   173	# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev
   174	# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
   175	# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
   176	```
   177	
   178	Automatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies
   179	still gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and
   180	`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left
   181	unchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the
   182	same dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `to-dev`,
   183	`development`, and `to-review`.
   184	
   185	`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do
   186	not select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being
   187	silently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container
   188	that still has a blocked descendant. The operator must reopen the container or
   189	resolve/close that descendant work.

